VALUES
  ('cs_san-francisco_history', 'plc_san-francisco', 'overview', 'History',
   'Etymology See also: List of San Francisco placename etymologies San Francisco, Spanish for ''Saint Francis'', takes its name from Mission San Francisco de Asís , which had been named in honor of Saint Francis of Assisi . [1] The mission received its name in 1776, when it was founded by the Spanish under the leadership of Padre Francisco Palóu . The city has officially been known as San Francisco since 1847, when Washington Allon Bartlett , then serving as the city''s alcalde , renamed it from Yerba Buena ('' good herb '' in Spanish), which had been the name of the first civilian pueblo in San Francisco. Earlier in San Francisco''s history, the uninhabited area on the northeastern side of San Francisco was called El Paraje de Yerba Buena , after the herb that was growing abundantly there. The name "Yerba Buena" continues to be used in locations in the city, such as on Yerba Buena Island . When using a nickname or abbreviation, local residents most commonly refer to San Francisco as "the City" or "SF". [2] [3] Although the nickname "Frisco" has a local pedigree dating at least to 1850 and has been used by some local residents in every generation since then, the uses of both "Frisco" and the historically more recent "San Fran" tend to elicit sharp divisions among residents. [3] [4] [5] The California Cantonese who came for the California Gold Rush named California and specifically San Francisco 金山 , Gāmsāan , " Gold Mountain ". When gold was discovered in Bendigo , Victoria, Australia, it was named "New Gold Mountain" ( Yue Chinese : 新金山 , Sān Gāmsāan ) and California and San Francisco as "Old Gold Mountain" ( 舊金山 , Gāu Gāmsāan ). [6] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "San Francisco de Asís – California Missions Foundation" . californiamissionsfoundation . Retrieved January 17, 2026 . ^ Cite error: The named reference TBI20130630 was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ a b Brinklow, Adam (January 26, 2018). "Is it ever okay to use "San Fran?" " . Curbed SF . Archived from the original on May 13, 2019 . Retrieved February 17, 2020 . ^ "Don''t Call It ''Frisco'': The History of San Francisco''s Nicknames" . The Bold Italic . December 20, 2019 . Retrieved February 17, 2020 . ^ Gilson, Dave (January 29, 2016). "Why San Francisco''s "Frisco" debate will never, ever die" . Mother Jones . Retrieved February 17, 2020 . ^ Yip, Liping Wong (2017). From Wah Lee to Chew Keen . FriesenPress. ISBN 978-1-4602-9430-7 . Retrieved March 24, 2025 . To distinguish between the two gold fie', '["clm_wiki_san-francisco_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   20, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
