  ('cs_san-francisco_demographics', 'plc_san-francisco', 'overview', 'Demographics',
   'Indigenous history The earliest archeological evidence of human habitation in the area dates to 3000 BCE. [1] The Ohlone name for San Francisco was Ahwaste , meaning "place at the bay". [2] The Yelamu group of the Ramaytush people lived in a few small villages when an overland Spanish exploration party arrived on November 2, 1769, the first documented European visit to San Francisco Bay . [3] The arrival of Spanish colonists, and the establishment of the Mission system, marked the beginning of the assimilation of the Ramaytush people and the decline of their language and culture. [4] [5] [6] ^ body:not( ):not( ) Stewart, Suzanne B. (November 2003). "Archaeological Research Issues for the Point Reyes National Seashore – Golden Gate National Recreation Area" (PDF) . Sonoma State University – Anthropological Studies Center. Archived from the original (PDF) on June 24, 2008 . Retrieved June 12, 2008 . ^ Billiter, Bill (January 1, 1985). "3,000-Year-Old Connection Claimed : Siberia Tie to California Tribes Cited" . Los Angeles Times . Retrieved September 25, 2023 . ^ "Visitors: San Francisco Historical Information" . City and County of San Francisco. n.d. Archived from the original on March 1, 2006 . Retrieved June 10, 2008 . ^ "Indigenous Peoples of San Mateo County" . San Mateo County Office of Education . Retrieved January 18, 2024 . ^ "The Association of Ramaytush Ohlone" . Retrieved January 18, 2024 . ^ "The Ramaytush Ohlone – Lessons on stewardship from the ancestral stewards of the Peninsula" . Golden Gate National Parks Conservancy . December 4, 2018 . Retrieved January 18, 2024 .', '["clm_wiki_san-francisco_demographics"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 19. Denver (slug=denver, wiki='Denver', type=city)
