VALUES
  ('cs_cleveland_geography', 'plc_cleveland', 'overview', 'Geography',
   'Late 20th and early 21st centuries Cleveland experienced an economic boom after the war and businesses declared the city to be the "best location in the nation". [1] [2] In 1950, its population reached 914,808. [3] The decade saw the rise of the rock and roll music genre, popularized by local disc jockey Alan Freed . [4] By the 1960s, however, Cleveland''s economy slowed and residents began seeking new housing in the suburbs, reflecting national trends of suburbanization following federally subsidized highways. [5] Industrial restructuring , particularly in the steel and automotive industries, resulted in the loss of numerous jobs in Cleveland and the city suffered economically. [6] The burning of the Cuyahoga River in June 1969 brought national attention to the issue of industrial pollution in Cleveland and catalyzed the American environmental movement . [7] Key Tower and the Fountain of Eternal Life by Marshall Fredericks Housing discrimination and redlining against African Americans led to racial unrest in Cleveland; [8] [9] the Hough riots erupted from July 18 to 24, 1966, [10] and the Glenville Shootout took place on July 23, 1968. [11] In November 1967, Cleveland became the first major American city to elect an African American mayor, Carl B. Stokes , who worked to restore the Cuyahoga River. [12] [13] During the 1970s, Cleveland was known as "Bomb City U.S.A." due to several bombings that shook the city, mostly due to organized crime rivalries. [14] In December 1978, Cleveland became the first major American city since the Great Depression to enter a financial default on federal loans. [15] The national recession of the early 1980s eroded the city''s traditional economic industries; [6] Cleveland''s unemployment rate of 13.8% was higher than the national average due to the closure of several steel production centers. [16] [17] The city began an economic recovery in the 1980s. Downtown saw the construction of the Key Tower and 200 Public Square skyscrapers, as we', '["clm_wiki_cleveland_geography"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
