VALUES
  ('cs_cleveland_demographics', 'plc_cleveland', 'overview', 'Demographics',
   'Mid to late 19th century Bird''s-eye view of Cleveland in 1877 A center of abolitionist activity, [1] [2] Cleveland (code-named "Station Hope") was a major stop on the Underground Railroad for escaped African American slaves en route to Canada. [3] The city served as an important center for the Union during the American Civil War , [4] [5] creating a large manufacturing industry within the city and unprecedented growth. [6] Its geographic location as a transportation hub on the Great Lakes contributed to its development as an industrial and commercial center. In 1870, John D. Rockefeller founded Standard Oil in Cleveland; [7] in 1885, he moved its headquarters to New York City . [8] Cleveland''s economic growth and industrial jobs attracted large waves of immigrants from Southern Europe , Eastern Europe , and Ireland . [9] Urban growth was accompanied by labor unrest as workers demanded better wages and working conditions . [10] The Cleveland Streetcar Strike of 1899 was one of the more violent instances of labor strife in the city during this period. [11] ^ Keating 2022 , pp. 13–15. ^ body:not( ):not( ) Wyatt-Brown, Bertram (May 31, 2019). "Abolitionism" . The Encyclopedia of Cleveland History . Case Western Reserve University . Retrieved June 7, 2020 . ^ "In Search of the Underground Railroad" . Cleveland Historical . Cleveland State University . Retrieved June 7, 2020 . ^ Keating 2022 , p. 38. ^ "Abraham Lincoln in Cleveland: Remembering a Slain President" . Cleveland Historical . Cleveland State University . Retrieved August 8, 2023 . ^ Miller &amp; Wheeler 1997 , p. 69 . ^ Rose 1990 , p. 937. ^ "American Experience: Rockefellers Timeline" . PBS . Retrieved May 19, 2023 . 1885: Standard Oil moves into new headquarters at 26 Broadway in New York. ^ Cite error: The named reference immigration-migration was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ Miller &amp; Wheeler 1997 , pp. 87–89 . ^ "Streetcar Strike of 1899" . The Encyclopedia of Cleveland History . C', '["clm_wiki_cleveland_demographics"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 27. Pittsburgh (slug=pittsburgh, wiki='Pittsburgh', type=city)
