VALUES
  ('cs_cincinnati_geography', 'plc_cincinnati', 'overview', 'Geography',
   'Nicknames Carew Tower , the city''s second-tallest building, and The Genius of Water , a symbol of Cincinnati &#91; citation needed &#93; Cincinnati has many nicknames , including Cincy, The Queen City, [1] The Queen of the West, [2] The Blue Chip City, [3] [4] [5] The City of Seven Hills, [6] and Porkopolis. &#91; citation needed &#93; "The City of Seven Hills " stems from the June 1853 edition of the West American Review , "Article III—Cincinnati: Its Relations to the West and South", which described and named seven specific hills. The hills form a crescent around the city: Mount Adams , Walnut Hills , Mount Auburn , Vine Street Hill , College Hill , Fairmont (now rendered Fairmount ), and Mount Harrison (now known as Price Hill ). The name refers to ancient Rome, reputed to be built on seven hills. &#91; citation needed &#93; "Queen City" is taken from an 1819 newspaper article [7] and further immortalized by the 1854 poem "Catawba Wine". In it, Henry Wadsworth Longfellow wrote of the city: And this Song of the Vine, This greeting of mine, The winds and the birds shall deliver, To the Queen of the West, In her garlands dressed, On the banks of the Beautiful River. [8] For many years, Cincinnati was also known as "Porkopolis" after the prominence of the pork industry in the city. [9] In 1988, the city built a park, Bicentennial Commons, to celebrate the 200th anniversary of the city''s founding and commissioned British artist Andrew Leicester to create a sculpture for the entrance. Leicester came up with four steamboat smokestacks, a nod to the city''s riverboat history, each topped with a winged pig. [10] Initial reaction was derision, but the city soon embraced the "Flying Pigs"; the city''s Flying Pig Marathon was named after them, and the city''s Big Pig Gig inspired by them. [10] [11] [12] The city has used it as a symbol and a brand. [10] [13] Newer nicknames such as "The ''Nati" are emerging and are attempted to be used in different cultural contexts. For example', '["clm_wiki_cincinnati_geography"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
