VALUES
  ('cs_milwaukee_geography', 'plc_milwaukee', 'overview', 'Geography',
   '20th century to present A slum area of Milwaukee from 1936 In 1910 , the seven most spoken languages in Milwaukee were German (167,108, or 44.7% of the population), English (104,892, or 28.06%), &#91; a &#93; Polish (58,109, or 15.54%), Yiddish (7,757, or 2.07%), Czech (6,370, or 1.7%), Norwegian (5,077, or 1.36%), and Italian (4,837, or 1.29%). [1] [2] By 2000 , the seven most spoken languages in Milwaukee were English (462,415, or 84.28% of the population), Spanish (55,245, or 10.07%), Hmong (7,340, or 1.34%), German (4,430, or 0.81%), French (2,240, or 0.41%), Polish (2,225, or 0.41%), and Arabic (1,555, or 0.28%). [3] During the first sixty years of the 20th century, Milwaukee was the major city in which the Socialist Party of America earned the highest votes. Milwaukee elected three mayors who ran on the ticket of the Socialist Party: Emil Seidel (1910–1912), Daniel Hoan (1916–1940), and Frank Zeidler (1948–1960), with Victor L. Berger elected as the first socialist congressman in 1910. Often referred to as " Sewer Socialists ", the Milwaukee Socialists were characterized by their practical approach to government and labor. [4] On October 14, 1912, former president Theodore Roosevelt , who ran for a third term under his progressive, Bull Moose Party , survived an assassination attempt by salonkeeper John Fleming Shrank , while making a speech at the Gilpatrick Hotel . On November 24, 1917, Milwaukee was the site of a terrorist explosion when a large black powder bomb [5] exploded at the central police station at Oneida and Broadway. [6] Nine members of the department were killed in the blast, along with a female civilian, Catherine Walker. [5] [7] It was suspected at the time that the bomb had been placed outside the church by anarchists, particularly the Galleanist faction led by adherents of Luigi Galleani . At the time, the bombing was the most fatal single event in national law enforcement history. [8] In the 1920s, Chicago gangster activity came north to M', '["clm_wiki_milwaukee_geography"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_milwaukee_demographics', 'plc_milwaukee', 'overview', 'Demographics',
   'Name The etymological origin of the name Milwaukee is disputed. [1] [2] Wisconsin academic Virgil J. Vogel has said, "the name [...] Milwaukee is not difficult to explain, yet there are a number of conflicting claims made concerning it." [3] One theory says it comes from the Anishinaabemowin/Ojibwe word mino-akking , meaning "good land", [1] [4] or words in closely related languages that mean the same. [5] These included Menominee and Potawatomi. [3] This theory was popularized by a line by Alice Cooper in the 1992 comedy film Wayne''s World . [1] Another theory is that it stems from the Meskwaki language, whose term for "gathering place" is mahn-a-waukee . [1] [4] The city of Milwaukee itself claims that the name is derived from mahn-ah-wauk , a Potawatomi word for "council grounds". [6] The name of the future city was spelled in many ways prior to 1844. [7] People living west of the Milwaukee River preferred the modern-day spelling, while those east of the river often called it Milwaukie . [1] Other spellings included Melleokii (1679), Millioki (1679), Meleki (1684), Milwarik (1699), Milwacky (1761), Milwakie (1779), Millewackie (1817), Milwahkie (1820), and Milwalky (1821). The Milwaukee Sentinel used Milwaukie in its headline until it switched to Milwaukee on November 30, 1844. [7] The city of Milwaukie, Oregon , retains the alternate spelling. ^ a b c d e body:not( ):not( ) Prigge, Matthew (January 29, 2018). "What Does ''Milwaukee'' Mean, Anyway?" . Milwaukee Magazine . Retrieved October 5, 2023 . ^ "Milwaukee County &#91;origin of place name&#93;" . Wisconsin Historical Society . August 8, 2017 . Retrieved October 5, 2023 . ^ a b Vogel, Virgil J. (1991). Indian Names on Wisconsin''s Map . Univ of Wisconsin Press. p. 34. ISBN 978-0-299-12984-2 . ^ a b "Mino-akking, Mahn-a-waukke: What''s The Origin Of The Word ''Milwaukee''?" . WUWM 89.7 FM - Milwaukee''s NPR . October 14, 2016 . Retrieved October 5, 2023 . ^ Bright, William (2004). Native American Placenames of the U', '["clm_wiki_milwaukee_demographics"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 35. Salt Lake City (slug=salt-lake-city, wiki='Salt Lake City', type=city)
