   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_anchorage_overview', 'plc_anchorage', 'overview', 'Overview',
   'Anchorage, officially the Municipality of Anchorage, is the most populous city in the U.S. state of Alaska. With a population of 291,247 at the 2020 census, it contains nearly 40 percent of the state''s population. The Anchorage metropolitan area, which includes Anchorage and the neighboring Matanuska-Susitna Borough, had a population of 398,328 in 2020, accounting for more than half the state''s population. At 1,706 sq mi (4,420 km2) of land area, the city is the fourth-largest by area in the U.S.', '["clm_wiki_anchorage_overview"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   10, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 39. Honolulu (slug=honolulu, wiki='Honolulu', type=city)
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_honolulu_overview', 'plc_honolulu', 'overview', 'Overview',
   'Honolulu is the capital and most populous city of the U.S. state of Hawaii, located in the Pacific Ocean. It is the county seat of the consolidated City and County of Honolulu, situated along the southeast coast of the island of Oʻahu. The population of Honolulu was 350,964 at the 2020 census, dropping to an estimated 341,868 by 2025. The Urban Honolulu metropolitan area had an estimated population of just under 1 million residents in 2024 and is the 56th-largest metropolitan area in the nation.', '["clm_wiki_honolulu_overview"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   10, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_honolulu_history', 'plc_honolulu', 'overview', 'History',
   'Etymology Honolulu is Hawaiian for ''sheltered harbor'' [1] or ''calm port''; [2] this former meaning is also reflected in the related Māori cognate toponym Whangaruru in the tip of New Zealand''s North Island . [3] [4] Its old name, Kou , roughly encompasses the area from Nuʻuanu Avenue to Alakea Street and from Hotel Street to Queen Street, which is the heart of the present downtown district. [5] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "About the City" . Honolulu . Archived from the original on February 1, 2015 . Retrieved October 21, 2014 . ^ "etymonline entry for Honolulu" . Retrieved August 27, 2013 . ^ Crowe, Andrew (May 2013). "New Zealand place names shared with the Hawaiian archipelago" . Rapa Nui . 27 (1). Easter Island Foundation: 21– 37. ^ Rāwiri Taonui (February 8, 2005). "Whāngārei tribes - Ancestors" . Te Ara: The Encyclopedia of New Zealand . Whangaruru (sheltered harbour), where the canoe found safety from bad weather... ^ "This Is Your City and County of Honolulu Government" . honolulu . Archived from the original on October 12, 2004 . Retrieved May 22, 2012 .', '["clm_wiki_honolulu_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   20, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_honolulu_geography', 'plc_honolulu', 'overview', 'Geography',
   'History For a chronological guide, see Timeline of Honolulu . Port of Honolulu, as seen by German-Russian artist Louis Choris in 1816 Great Chinatown Fire of 1900 View of the attack on Pearl Harbor in 1941 Evidence of the first settlement of Honolulu by the original Polynesian migrants to the archipelago comes from oral histories and artifacts. These indicate that there was a settlement where Honolulu now stands in the 11th century. [1] &#91; unreliable source? &#93; After Kamehameha I conquered Oʻahu in the Battle of Nuʻuanu at Nuʻuanu Pali , he moved his royal court from the Island of Hawaiʻi to Waikiki in 1804. His court relocated in 1809 to what is now downtown Honolulu . The capital was moved back to Kailua-Kona in 1812. In November 1794, Captain William Brown of Great Britain was the first foreigner to sail into what is now Honolulu Harbor . [2] More foreign ships followed, making the port of Honolulu a focal point for merchant ships traveling between North America and Asia. The settlement grew from a handful of homes to a city in the early 19th century after Kamehameha I chose it as a replacement for his residence at Waikiki in 1810. [3] In 1845, Kamehameha III moved the permanent capital of the Hawaiian Kingdom from Lahaina on Maui to Honolulu. [3] He and the kings who followed him transformed Honolulu into a modern capital, erecting buildings such as St. Andrew''s Cathedral , ʻIolani Palace , and Aliʻiōlani Hale . At the same time, Honolulu became the islands'' center of commerce, with descendants of American missionaries establishing major businesses downtown. [4] Despite the turbulent history of the late 19th century and early 20th century—such as the overthrow of the Hawaiian monarchy in 1893, Hawaii''s annexation by the U.S. in 1898, a large fire in 1900, and the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor in 1941—Honolulu remained the Hawaiian Islands'' capital, largest city, and main airport and seaport. [5] An economic and tourism boom following statehood brought ra', '["clm_wiki_honolulu_geography"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
  (id, entity_id, section_type, heading, body_markdown, claim_ids, source_ids,
   reading_level, language, author_type, generator_version, editorial_status,
   version, created_at, updated_at, display_order, claim_count, source_count, last_verified_at)
VALUES
  ('cs_honolulu_demographics', 'plc_honolulu', 'overview', 'Demographics',
   'Climate Honolulu experiences a hot semi-arid climate ( Köppen classification BSh ), with a mostly dry summer season, due to a rain shadow effect. [1] Despite temperatures that meet the tropical threshold of all months having a mean temperature of 64.4 °F (18.0 °C) or higher, the city receives too little precipitation to be classified as tropical. Temperatures vary little throughout the year, with average high temperatures of 80–90 °F (27–32 °C) and average lows of 65–75 °F (18–24 °C). Nevertheless, there are slight seasons. The "winter" months from December to March can occasionally see lows fall below 67 °F (19 °C), whereas the "summer" from June to September can get a limited number of hot days achieving 90 °F (32 °C) or higher. This occurs on an average of only 32 days annually, [2] &#91; a &#93; with lows in the upper 50s °F (14–15 °C) once or twice a year. The highest recorded temperature was 95 °F (35 °C) on September 19, 1994, and August 31, 2019. [2] The lowest recorded temperature was 52 °F (11 °C) on February 16, 1902, and January 20, 1969. [2] The annual average rainfall is 16.41 inches (417 millimeters), [2] which mainly occurs from October through early April, with very little rainfall in the summer. However, both seasons experience a similar number of rainy days. Light showers occur in summer, while heavier rain falls during winter. Honolulu has an average of 278 sunny days and 89.2 rainy days per year. Although the city is in the tropics, hurricanes are quite rare. The last recorded hurricane that hit near Honolulu was Category 4 Hurricane Iniki in 1992. Tornadoes are also uncommon and occur about every 15 years. Waterspouts off the coast are also uncommon, hitting about every five years. [3] Honolulu falls under the USDA 12b Plant Hardiness zone . [4] The average temperature of the sea ranges from 75.7 °F (24.3 °C) in March to 80.4 °F (26.9 °C) in September. [5] Climate data for Honolulu International Airport (1991−2020 normals, &#91; b &#93; extreme', '["clm_wiki_honolulu_demographics"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 40. Tucson (slug=tucson, wiki='Tucson', type=city)
