VALUES
  ('cs_independence-national-historical-park_demographics', 'plc_independence-national-historical-park', 'overview', 'Demographics',
   'Philadelphia Convention Further information: Philadelphia Convention Scene at the Signing of the Constitution of the United States , a 1940 portrait by Howard Chandler Christy After 1781, the national government operated under the Articles of Confederation , which gave the federal government virtually no power to regulate domestic affairs or raise revenue. [1] At the Annapolis Convention in September 1786, the delegates asked for a broader meeting to be held the next May in Philadelphia to address the regulation of trade and the structure of the government. [2] This resulted in the Philadelphia Convention , which met from May 14 to September 17, 1787, at the Pennsylvania State House. [3] The convention was dominated by controversies and conflicting interests, but the delegates forged a Constitution that has been called a "bundle of compromises". [4] At the convention, delegate James Madison presented the Virginia Plan , which proposed a national government with three branches with proportional representation . [5] Large states supported this plan, but smaller states feared losing substantial power under the plan. In response, William Paterson designed the New Jersey Plan , which proposed a one-house ( unicameral ) legislature in which each state, regardless of size, would have one vote, as under the Articles of Confederation. [6] Roger Sherman combined the two plans with the Connecticut Compromise , and his measure passed on July 16, 1787, by seven to six—a margin of one vote. [7] Other contentious issues were slavery and the federal regulation of commerce, which resulted in additional compromises. ^ The Constitutional Convention of 1787 , University of Missouri-Kansas City School of Law. ^ body:not( ):not( ) Bloom, Sol; Johnson, Lars (2001). The Story of the Constitution . Arlington Heights, IL: Christian Liberty Press. p. 40. ISBN 1-930367-56-2 . Retrieved May 29, 2011 . ^ Bloom, Sol; Johnson, Lars (2001). The Story of the Constitution . Arlington Heights, IL: Chr', '["clm_wiki_independence-national-historical-park_demographics"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 72. Fenway Park (slug=fenway-park, wiki='Fenway Park', type=landmark)
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO content_section
