  ('cs_fenway-park_history', 'plc_fenway-park', 'overview', 'History',
   'Baseball stadium in Boston, Massachusetts "Fenway" redirects here. For other uses, see Fenway (disambiguation) . Fenway Park America''s Most Beloved Ballpark [1] Friendly Fenway Fenway Park during the 2013 World Series Fenway Park Location in Boston Show map of Boston Fenway Park Location in Massachusetts Show map of Massachusetts Fenway Park Location in the United States Show map of the United States Address 4 Jersey Street [2] Location Boston , Massachusetts, U.S. Coordinates 42°20′47″N 71°5′52″W &#xfeff; / &#xfeff; 42.34639°N 71.09778°W &#xfeff; / 42.34639; -71.09778 Owner Fenway Sports Group Operator Fenway Sports Group / Boston Red Sox Capacity 37,305 (day) 37,755 (night) [3] Surface Kentucky Blue Grass Record attendance 47,627 (September 22, 1935) [4] Field size Left Field : 310 ft (94.5 m) Deep Left-Center : 379 ft (115.5 m) Center Field : 389 ft 9 in (118.8 m) Deep Right-Center : 420 ft (128 m) Right Center : 380 ft (115.8 m) Right Field : 302 ft (92 m) Backstop : 60 ft (18.3 m) Public transit Framingham/Worcester Line at Lansdowne Green&#32;Line at Kenmore and Fenway Construction Groundbreaking September 25, 1911 ( 1911-09-25 ) Opened April 20, 1912 ( 1912-04-20 ) Renovated 1988, 2002–2011, 2017 Expanded 1934, 1946, 2002–2011, 2017, 2022 Cost US$650,000 ($21.7 million in 2025 dollars [5] ) Architect James E. McLaughlin [6] [7] Structural engineer Osborn Engineering Corp. [7] General contractor Charles Logue Building Company, Coleman Brothers, Inc. [7] Tenants Boston Red Sox ( MLB ) (1912–present) Boston Braves ( MLB ) (1914–1915) Boston Bulldogs ( AFL ) (1926) Boston Redskins ( NFL ) (1933–1936) Boston Shamrocks ( AFL ) (1936–1937) Boston Yanks ( NFL ) (1944–1948) Boston Patriots ( AFL ) (1963–1968) Boston Beacons ( NASL ) (1968) Fenway Bowl ( NCAA ) (2022–present) Website mlb /redsox/ballpark Fenway Park U.S. National Register of Historic Places NRHP reference No. 12000069 [8] Added to NRHP March 7, 2012 Preview warning: Page using Template:Infobox NRHP with deprecated parameter refnum . Replace with ref_num . View of Fenway Park from atop the Green Monster Fenway Park is a ballpark in Boston , Massachusetts, United States, close to Kenmore Square . Since 1912, it has been the home field of Major League Baseball ''s (MLB) Boston Red Sox . While the stadium was built in 1912, it was substantially rebuilt in 1934, [9] and underwent major renovations and modifications in the 21st century. It is the oldest active ballpark in MLB. [10] Because of its ag', '["clm_wiki_fenway-park_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
