VALUES
  ('cs_times-square_history', 'plc_times-square', 'overview', 'History',
   'Geography Times Square functions as a town square , but is not geometrically a square . It is closer in shape to a bowtie, with two triangles emanating roughly north and south from 45th Street, [1] where Seventh Avenue intersects Broadway . Broadway similarly intersects other north–south avenues at Union Square , Madison Square , Herald Square , and Verdi Square , none of which are squares. [2] The area is bounded by West 42nd Street , West 47th Street , 7th Avenue, and Broadway. Broadway runs diagonally, crossing through the horizontal and vertical street grid of Manhattan laid down by the Commissioners'' Plan of 1811 , and that intersection creates the "bowtie" shape of Times Square. [3] [4] Times Square is the official name of the southern triangle, below 45th Street. [5] The northern triangle is officially known as Duffy Square [6] and was dedicated in June 1939 to honor World War I chaplain Father Francis P. Duffy of the 69th New York Infantry Regiment . [7] A statue by Charles Keck was dedicated in May 1937 as a memorial to Duffy. [8] There is also a statue of composer and entertainer George M. Cohan , [9] [10] and the TKTS discount ticket booth for same-day Broadway and off-Broadway theaters that has been at the site since June 1973. [11] [12] ^ "Times Square is not a square," Archived June 8, 2022, at the Wayback Machine Urban Magazine (May 9, 2011). ^ body:not( ):not( ) Kimmelman, Michael (January 3, 2012). "The Grid at 200: Lines That Shaped Manhattan" . The New York Times . ISSN 0362-4331 . Archived from the original on March 21, 2022 . Retrieved March 21, 2022 . ^ Rybczynski, Witold . City Life: Urban Expectations in a New World New York: Scribner, 1995. p.27. ISBN 0-684-81302-5 . Quote: ".. its name [Times Square] is really a street intersection, not a square." ^ Dunlap, David W. "COMMERCIAL PROPERTY: Times Square Novelty; North of Duffy Sq., a Butler-on-Every-Floor Hotel" Archived December 13, 2021, at the Wayback Machine , The New York Times , June 10, 1990. Accessed February 26, 2017. "With its hot nightclubs, dazzling signs and sprawling showrooms, the block at the northern end of the ''bowtie'' formed by Broadway and Seventh Avenue was always an important part of old Times Square." ^ "Times Square" Archived January 10, 2022, at the Wayback Machine New York City Geographic Information Service map ^ Father Duffy Square Archived January 12, 2017, at the Wayback Machine , New York City Department of Parks and Recreation . Accessed January 10, 20', '["clm_wiki_times-square_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   20, 1, 1, unixepoch());
