VALUES
  ('cs_brooklyn-bridge_demographics', 'plc_brooklyn-bridge', 'overview', 'Demographics',
   'Suspension span The main span between the two suspension towers is 1,595.5 feet (486.3 m) long and 85 feet (26 m) wide. [1] [2] [3] The bridge "elongates and contracts between the extremes of temperature from 14 to 16 inches". [4] Navigational clearance is 127 ft (38.7 m) above mean high water (MHW). [5] [6] A 1909 Engineering Magazine article said that, at the center of the span, the height above MHW could fluctuate by more than 9 feet (2.7 m) due to temperature and traffic loads, while more rigid spans had a lower maximum deflection . [7] The side spans, between each suspension tower and each side''s suspension anchorages, are 930 feet (280 m) long. [1] [2] At the time of construction, engineers had not yet discovered the aerodynamics of bridge construction, and bridge designs were not tested in wind tunnels . John Roebling designed the Brooklyn Bridge''s truss system to be six to eight times as strong as he thought it needed to be. As such, the open truss structure supporting the deck is, by its nature, subject to fewer aerodynamic problems. [8] [9] However, due to a supplier''s fraudulent substitution of inferior-quality wire in the initial construction, the bridge was reappraised at the time as being only four times as strong as necessary. [8] [10] The main span and side spans are supported by a structure containing trusses that run parallel to the roadway, [11] each of which is 33 feet (10 m) deep. [12] [13] Originally there were six trusses, [11] but two were removed during a late-1940s renovation. [14] [15] The trusses allow the Brooklyn Bridge to hold a total load of 18,700 short tons (17,000 metric tons), a design consideration from when it originally carried heavier elevated trains. [16] [17] These trusses are held up by suspender ropes, which hang downward from each of the four main cables. Crossbeams run between the trusses at the top, and diagonal and vertical stiffening beams run on the outside and inside of each roadway. [12] [13] An elevated pedestrian', '["clm_wiki_brooklyn-bridge_demographics"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
-- 76. Wall Street (slug=wall-street, wiki='Wall Street', type=landmark)
