  ('cs_metropolitan-museum-of-art_demographics', 'plc_metropolitan-museum-of-art', 'overview', 'Demographics',
   'Ancient Near Eastern art Beginning in the late 19th century, the Met started acquiring ancient art and artifacts from the Near East . From a few cuneiform tablets and seals , the museum''s collection of Near Eastern art has grown to more than 7,000 pieces. [1] Representing a history of the region beginning in the Neolithic Period and encompassing the fall of the Sasanian Empire and the end of Late Antiquity , the collection includes works from the Sumerian , Hittite , Sasanian, Assyrian , Babylonian , and Elamite cultures (among others), as well as an extensive collection of unique Bronze Age objects. The highlights of the collection include the Sumerian Stele of Ushumgal , the Elamite silver Kneeling Bull with Vessel , the Pratt Ivories , and a set of monumental stone lamassu , or guardian figures, from the Northwest Palace of the Assyrian king Ashurnasirpal II . [2] ^ body:not( ):not( ) "The Metropolitan Museum of Art – Ancient Near Eastern Art" . Metmuseum . Archived from the original on May 30, 2012 . Retrieved June 11, 2012 . ^ "Assyria, 1365–609 B.C. &#124; Thematic Essay &#124; Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History &#124; The Metropolitan Museum of Art" . Metmuseum . October 2004. Archived from the original on July 4, 2012 . Retrieved June 11, 2012 .', '["clm_wiki_metropolitan-museum-of-art_demographics"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
