  ('cs_princeton-university_history', 'plc_princeton-university', 'overview', 'History',
   'Private university in Princeton, New Jersey, US "Princeton" redirects here. For the municipality in New Jersey, see Princeton, New Jersey . For the public college, see The College of New Jersey . For other uses, see Princeton (disambiguation) . Princeton University Latin : Universitas Princetoniensis [1] Former name College of New Jersey (1746–1896) Motto Dei Sub Numine Viget ( Latin ) [2] On seal : Vet[us] Nov[um] Testamentum (Latin) Motto in English "Under God''s Power She Flourishes" [2] On seal : "Old Testament and New Testament" Type Private research university Established October 22, 1746 &#59;&#32;279 years ago ( 1746-10-22 ) Accreditation MSCHE Academic affiliations AAU COFHE NAICU URA space-grant Endowment $36.4 billion (2025) [3] Budget [4] President Christopher L. Eisgruber Provost Jennifer Rexford Faculty 1,128 (fall 2024) [5] Total staff 7,300 [6] Students 9,137 (fall 2024) [5] Undergraduates 5,813 (fall 2024) [5] Postgraduates 3,324 (fall 2024) [5] Doctoral students 2,631 [7] Location Princeton, New Jersey , United States 40°20′43″N 74°39′22″W &#xfeff; / &#xfeff; 40.34528°N 74.65611°W &#xfeff; / 40.34528; -74.65611 [8] Campus 600 acres (2.4 km 2 ) [6] Small city Newspaper The Daily Princetonian Colors Black and orange [9] Nickname Tigers Sporting affiliations NCAA Division I FCS – Ivy League ECAC Hockey EARC EIVA CWPA IRA EAWRC Mascot The Tiger Website princeton Princeton University is a private Ivy League research university in Princeton, New Jersey , United States. Founded in 1746 in Elizabeth as the College of New Jersey , Princeton is the fourth-oldest institution of higher education in the United States and one of the nine colonial colleges chartered before the American Revolution . [10] [11] &#91; a &#93; The institution moved to Newark in 1747 and then to its Mercer County campus in Princeton nine years later. It officially became a university in 1896 and was subsequently renamed Princeton University. The university is governed by the Trustees of Princeton University and has an endowment of $37.7 billion, the largest endowment per student in the United States. Princeton provides undergraduate and graduate instruction in the humanities , social sciences , natural sciences , and engineering to approximately 9,000 students on its main campus spanning 600 acres (2.4 km 2 ) within the borough of Princeton. It offers postgraduate degrees through the Princeton School of Public and International Affairs , the School of Engineering and Applied S', '["clm_wiki_princeton-university_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
