VALUES
  ('cs_princeton-university_geography', 'plc_princeton-university', 'overview', 'Geography',
   'Founding The Log College , an influential aspect of Princeton''s development Princeton University, founded as the College of New Jersey, was shaped much in its formative years by the " Log College ", a seminary founded by the Reverend William Tennent at Neshaminy, Pennsylvania , in about 1726. While no legal connection ever existed, many of the pupils and adherents from the Log College would go on to financially support and become substantially involved in the early years of the university. [1] While early writers considered it as the predecessor of the university, [2] the idea has been rebuked by Princeton historians. [3] [1] The founding of the university itself originated from a split in the Presbyterian church following the Great Awakening . [4] In 1741, New Light Presbyterians were expelled from the Synod of Philadelphia in defense of how the Log College ordained ministers. [5] The four founders of the College of New Jersey, who were New Lights, were either expelled or withdrew from the Synod and devised a plan to establish a new college, for they were disappointed with Harvard and Yale ''s opposition to the Great Awakening and dissatisfied with the limited instruction at the Log College. [5] [4] They convinced three other Presbyterians to join them and decided on New Jersey as the location for the college, as at the time, there was no institution between Yale College in New Haven, Connecticut , and the College of William &amp; Mary in Williamsburg, Virginia ; it was also where some of the founders preached. [6] Although their initial request was rejected by the Anglican governor Lewis Morrison , the acting governor after Morrison''s death, John Hamilton , granted a charter for the College of New Jersey on October 22, 1746. [7] [6] In 1747, approximately five months after acquiring the charter, the trustees elected Jonathan Dickinson as president and opened in Elizabeth, New Jersey , [7] where classes were held in Dickinson''s parsonage . [8] With its founding, it ', '["clm_wiki_princeton-university_geography"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
