VALUES
  ('cs_princeton-university_demographics', 'plc_princeton-university', 'overview', 'Demographics',
   'Colonial and early years From 1760, the first picture of Nassau Hall In 1747, following the death of then President Jonathan Dickinson, the college moved from Elizabeth to Newark, New Jersey , as that was where presidential successor Aaron Burr Sr. ''s parsonage was located. [1] That same year, Princeton''s first charter came under dispute by Anglicans, but on September 14, 1748, the recently appointed governor Jonathan Belcher granted a second charter. [2] [3] Belcher, a Congregationalist , had become alienated from his alma mater , Harvard, and decided to "adopt" the infant college. [2] [4] Belcher would go on to raise funds for the college and donate his 474-volume library, making it one of the largest libraries in the colonies. [2] [5] John Witherspoon , president of the college (1768–94) and signer of the Declaration of Independence In 1756, the college moved again to its present home in Princeton, New Jersey , because Newark was felt to be too close to New York. [6] [7] Princeton was chosen for its location in central New Jersey and by strong recommendation by Belcher. [2] [8] The college''s home in Princeton was Nassau Hall , named for the royal William III of England , a member of the House of Orange-Nassau . [9] The trustees of the College of New Jersey initially suggested that Nassau Hall be named in recognition of Belcher because of his interest in the institution; the governor vetoed the request. [2] Burr, who would die in 1757, devised a curriculum for the school and enlarged the student body. [10] Following the untimely death of Burr and the college''s next three presidents , [11] John Witherspoon became president in 1768 and remained in that post until his death in 1794. [12] With his presidency, Witherspoon focused the college on preparing a new generation of both educated clergy and secular leadership in the new American nation. [13] [14] To this end, he tightened academic standards, broadened the curriculum, solicited investment for the college, and gr', '["clm_wiki_princeton-university_demographics"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
