VALUES
  ('cs_university-of-michigan_demographics', 'plc_university-of-michigan', 'overview', 'Demographics',
   'The Catholepistemiad, 1817–1821 From top to bottom: Ste. Anne''s "Stone Church", built in 1818; The Catholepistemiad''s schoolhouse (center) on an 1818 map of Detroit. The schoolhouse fronts Bates Street, directly across from Ste. Anne''s "Stone Church" (middle of the lower right). The university was modeled after an institution established in France a decade earlier, known as the Université imperial , under Emperor Napoleon . [1] [2] The term "Catholepistemiad", a neologism derived from a blend of Greek and Latin roots, can be loosely translated as "School of Universal Knowledge". [3] This entity included an array of schools and libraries under a single administration, [4] with the authority to establish additional schools across the territory. [1] [2] It was not until the legislative council passed the territory''s first public school law on April 12, 1827, which made basic education a municipal duty, that the corporation focused solely on higher education. The university may have initially been funded through private donations and federal land grants. [5] Shortly after its founding in 1817, it received a $250 subscription from the Freemason Zion Lodge of Detroit . [5] Of the total amount subscribed to establish the university, two-thirds came from the Zion Lodge and its members. [5] [6] Early benefactors included Joseph Campau (1769–1863) and his nephew John R. Williams (1782–1854). The first endowment may have been a land grant from the U.S. federal government on May 20, 1826, as part of "an Act concerning a seminary of learning in the territory of Michigan," based on the Treaty at the Foot of the Rapids and the Treaty at Detroit . [5] [7] The cornerstone of the University Building, located on Bates Street directly across from the 1818 "Stone Church" of Ste. Anne Parish in Detroit, [8] [9] was laid on September 24, 1817. [6] By the following year, a Lancasterian school , taught by Lemuel Shattuck , [10] and a classical academy, taught by Hugh M. Dickey, [9] were in ', '["clm_wiki_university-of-michigan_demographics"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
