VALUES
  ('cs_university-of-california-berkeley_geography', 'plc_university-of-california-berkeley', 'overview', 'Geography',
   'Founding Made possible by President Lincoln ''s signing of the Morrill Act in 1862, the University of California was founded in 1868 as the state''s first land-grant university, inheriting the land and facilities of the private College of California and the federal-funding eligibility of a public agricultural, mining, and mechanical arts college. [1] The Organic Act states that the "University shall have for its design, to provide instruction and thorough and complete education in all departments of science, literature and art, industrial and professional pursuits, and general education, and also special courses of instruction in preparation for the professions." [2] [3] Ten faculty members and forty male students made up the fledgling university when it opened in Oakland in 1869. [4] Frederick Billings , a trustee of the College of California , suggested that a new campus site north of Oakland be named in honor of Anglo-Irish philosopher George Berkeley . [5] The university began admitting women the following year. [6] In 1870, Henry Durant , founder of the College of California, became its first president. With the completion of North and South Halls in 1873, the university relocated to its Berkeley location with 167 male and 22 female students. [7] [8] The first female student to graduate was in 1874, admitted in the first class to include women in 1870. [9] Beginning in 1891, Phoebe Apperson Hearst funded several programs and new buildings and, in 1898, sponsored an international competition in Antwerp , where French architect Émile Bénard submitted the winning design for a campus master plan. ^ body:not( ):not( ) Stadtman, Verne A. (1970). The University of California, 1868–1968 . New York: McGraw-Hill. p. 34 . ^ "History of UC Berkeley" . University of California, Berkeley. Archived from the original on November 23, 2010. Founded in the wake of the gold rush by leaders of the newly established 31st state, the University of California''s flagship campus at Berkele', '["clm_wiki_university-of-california-berkeley_geography"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());
