VALUES
  ('cs_georgia-tech_demographics', 'plc_georgia-tech', 'overview', 'Demographics',
   'Early years An early picture of Georgia Tech, circa 1899 The Georgia School of Technology opened in the fall of 1888 with two buildings. [1] One building (now Tech Tower , an administrative headquarters) had classrooms to teach students. The second building featured a shop and had a foundry , forge , boiler room , and engine room. It was designed for students to work and produce goods to sell and fund the school. The two buildings were equal in size to show the importance of teaching both the mind and the hands, though, at the time, there was some disagreement as to whether the machine shop should have been used to turn a profit. [1] [2] Georgia Tech''s Evening School of Commerce began holding classes in 1912. [3] The evening school admitted its first female student in 1917, although the state legislature did not officially authorize attendance by women until 1920. [3] [4] Annie T. Wise became the first female graduate in 1919 and was Georgia Tech''s first female faculty member the following year. [3] [4] In 1931, the Board of Regents transferred control of the Evening School of Commerce to the University of Georgia (UGA) and moved the civil and electrical engineering courses at UGA to Georgia Tech. [3] [4] Georgia Tech replaced the commerce school with what later became the College of Business . The commerce school would later split from UGA and eventually became Georgia State University . [3] [5] In 1934, the Engineering Experiment Station (later known as the Georgia Tech Research Institute ) was founded by W. Harry Vaughan with an initial budget of $5,000 (equivalent to $120,336&#32;in 2025) and 13 part-time faculty. [6] [7] In the 1940s, President Blake Van Leer focused on making Georgia Tech the " MIT of the South." [8] He lobbied government and businesses for funds for new facilities. The Research Building was expanded, and a $300,000 (equivalent to $4,000,000&#32;in 2025) Westinghouse A-C network calculator was given to Georgia Tech by Georgia Power in 1947. [9', '["clm_wiki_georgia-tech_demographics"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
