  ('cs_carnegie-mellon-university_history', 'plc_carnegie-mellon-university', 'overview', 'History',
   'University in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, US Carnegie Mellon University Former names Carnegie Technical Schools (1900–1912) Carnegie Institute of Technology (1912–1967) Mellon Institute of Industrial Research (1913–1967) Motto "My heart is in the work" – Andrew Carnegie Type Private research university Established November 15, 1900 &#59;&#32;125 years ago ( 1900-11-15 ) [1] Founders Andrew Carnegie Andrew Mellon Richard B. Mellon Accreditation MSCHE Academic affiliations AAU AITU NAICU ORAU URA Space-grant Endowment $4.29 billion (2025) [2] President Farnam Jahanian Provost James Garrett Faculty 1,483 [3] Students 16,676 (2024) [4] Undergraduates 7,744 (2024) [4] Postgraduates 8,932 (2024) [4] Location Pittsburgh , Pennsylvania , United States 40°26′33″N 79°56′36″W &#xfeff; / &#xfeff; 40.44250°N 79.94333°W &#xfeff; / 40.44250; -79.94333 Campus 157.2 acres (63.6 ha) [5] Large city [6] Other campuses US: Los Angeles [7] Mountain View New York City [8] Washington [9] International: Doha Kigali Online Newspaper The Tartan Colors Red, black, steel gray, and iron gray [10] [11] Nickname Tartans Sporting affiliations NCAA Division III – UAA Centennial ACHA IRA [12] Mascot Scotty the Scottish Terrier [13] Website cmu Carnegie Mellon University ( CMU ) is a private research university in Pittsburgh , Pennsylvania, United States. The institution was established in 1900 by Andrew Carnegie as the Carnegie Technical Schools . In 1912, it became the Carnegie Institute of Technology and began granting four-year degrees. In 1967, it became Carnegie Mellon University through its merger with the Mellon Institute of Industrial Research , founded in 1913 by Andrew Mellon and Richard B. Mellon and formerly a part of the University of Pittsburgh . [14] The university consists of seven colleges, including the College of Engineering , the School of Computer Science , the Dietrich College of Humanities and Social Sciences , and the Tepper School of Business . [15] The university has its main campus located five miles (eight kilometers) from downtown Pittsburgh . It also has over a dozen degree-granting locations on six continents, including campuses in Qatar , Silicon Valley , and Kigali, Rwanda ( Carnegie Mellon University Africa ). [16] In 2022, Carnegie Mellon enrolled 15,818 students across its multiple campuses from 117 countries and employed more than 1,400 faculty members. [17] Carnegie Mellon is a member of the Association of American Universities and is classified among "R1', '["clm_wiki_carnegie-mellon-university_history"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
