VALUES
  ('cs_fr-country_demographics', 'ent_fr', 'overview', 'Demographics',
   'Prehistory Main article: Prehistory of France The oldest traces of archaic humans in what is now France date from approximately 1.8 million years ago. [1] Neanderthals occupied the region into the Upper Paleolithic era but were slowly replaced by Homo sapiens around 35,000 BC. [2] This period witnessed the emergence of cave painting in the Dordogne and Pyrenees , including at Lascaux , dated to c. &#8201;18,000 BC. [1] At the end of the Last Glacial Period (10,000 BC), the climate became milder. [1] From approximately 7,000 BC, this part of Western Europe entered the Neolithic era, and its inhabitants became sedentary . After demographic and agricultural development between the 4th and 3rd millennia BC, metallurgy appeared , initially working gold, copper and bronze , then later iron . [3] France has numerous megalithic sites from the Neolithic, including the Carnac stones site (approximately 3,300 BC). ^ a b c Jean Carpentier (dir.), François Lebrun (dir.), Alain Tranoy, Élisabeth Carpentier et Jean-Marie Mayeur (préface de Jacques Le Goff), Histoire de France, Points Seuil, coll. " Histoire ", Paris, 2000 (1re éd. 1987), p. 17. body:not( ):not( ) ISBN 978-2-02-010879-9 . ^ Jenkins, C. (2011). "Cro-Magnon Man, Roman Gaul and the Feudal Kingdom". A Brief History of France . Little, Brown Book Group . p. 6. ISBN 978-1849018128 . Archived from the original on 19 April 2023 . Retrieved 19 April 2023 . ^ Carpentier et al. 2000, pp. 20–24.', '["clm_wiki_fr-country_demographics"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
