  ('cs_united-states_demographics', 'ent_usa', 'overview', 'Demographics',
   'Indigenous peoples Main articles: History of Native Americans in the United States and Pre-Columbian era Cliff Palace , a settlement of ancestors of the Native American Pueblo peoples in present-day Montezuma County, Colorado , built between c. &#8201;1200 and 1275 [1] The first inhabitants of North America migrated from Siberia approximately 15,000 years ago, either across the Bering land bridge or along the now-submerged Ice Age coastline . [2] [3] The Clovis culture , which appeared around 11,000 BCE in North America, is believed to be the first widespread culture in the Americas. [4] [5] Over time, Indigenous North American cultures grew increasingly sophisticated, and some, such as the Mississippian culture , developed agriculture , architecture , and complex societies . [6] In the post-archaic period , the Mississippian cultures were located in the midwestern , eastern , and southern regions, and the Algonquian in the Great Lakes region and along the Eastern Seaboard , while the Hohokam culture and Ancestral Puebloans inhabited the Southwest . [7] Native population estimates of what is now the United States before the arrival of European colonizers range from around 500,000 [8] [9] to nearly 10 million. [9] [10] ^ "Cliff Palace" at Colorado Encyclopedia. Retrieved January 31, 2024. ^ Erlandson, Rick &amp; Vellanoweth 2008 , p. 19. ^ Savage 2011 , pp. 55–58. ^ Waters &amp; Stafford 2007 , pp. 1122–1126. ^ Flannery 2015 , pp. 173–185. ^ Lockard 2010 , p. 315. ^ Johansen 2006 , p. 51. ^ Thornton 1998 , p. 34. ^ a b Perdue &amp; Green 2005 , p. 40. ^ Haines, Haines &amp; Steckel 2000 , p. 12.', '["clm_wiki_united-states_demographics"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'place_sections_v2', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   40, 1, 1, unixepoch());
